  "finalReply": "Done. Everything is in `/app`:\n\n- **`cli_tool`** (binary, 155K): run as `./cli_tool weights.json image.png`, prints only the predicted digit. Implements the `MnistModel` forward pass (Linear 784→16, ReLU, Linear 16→16, ReLU, Linear 16→10, argmax) in C, using lodepng for PNG decoding (any color type, bilinear resize if not 28×28, luminance → /255.0) and cJSON for weight loading.\n- **`weights.json`** (280K): model weights exported from `model.pth` (stdlib-only `zipfile`/`struct` extraction, since torch isn't installable here): `input_dim=784, hidden_dim=16, num_classes=10` plus the 3 weight matrices (row-major, matching `nn.Linear`) and 3 bias vectors.\n- **`prediction.txt`**: contains `2` (output of `./cli_tool weights.json image.png`).\n- Source kept at `/app/cli_tool.c` (built with `g++ -O2 cli_tool.c lodepng.cpp cJSON.c -o cli_tool -lm`).\n\nVerification performed:\n- `./cli_tool weights.json image.png` → `2`, matching an independent pure-Python forward pass on the extracted weights (both `/255` and MNIST-mean/std normalizations predict 2; tool uses standard `/255`).\n- Cross-checked C vs Python on synthetic inputs: all-black → `9`, all-white → `7` (both agree); RGB-white equals gray-white; non-28×28 resize path works.\n- Usage errors (`0`/`1` args) exit 1 with a usage message on stderr; stdout contains only the digit.",
